    splashText = "COMMON INSTRUCTIONS\nCreate one textless 1920x1080 concept-sampler image for BlackCaesar-9, divided into four equal 16:9 quads in a clean 2x2 grid: Quad A top-left, Quad B top-right, Quad C bottom-left, and Quad D bottom-right. Each quad must occupy exactly one quarter of the canvas and depict a different unified frozen moment suitable for later expansion into a full 1920x1080 Memoriam splash. Use a narrow neutral-black cross-shaped gutter; do not let figures, architecture, smoke, weather, ships, or lighting cross between quads.\n\nREFERENCE INPUTS — Attach these files as actual image inputs. Attach `assets/references/style-references/general-style-reference-contact-sheet.png` only as the rendering and composition guide: cinematic gothic-noir realism, near-monochrome black, charcoal, and cold silver, hard contrast, deep negative space, wet or metallic highlights, layered atmospheric depth, and restrained blood-red accents. Do not copy its characters, Toronto locations, symbols, readable words, or exact compositions. Attach `assets/references/pc-character-references/Black Caesar [Cutout].webp` as the exact identity reference for Black Caesar; preserve his facial structure, dark skin, powerful build, apparent adult age, and commanding presence while replacing modern clothing and background with historically appropriate content. Attach `assets/references/npc-character-references/Jervais de la Croix [Cutout].webp` as exact identity reference for Jervais de la Croix; preserve each named figure’s face, apparent age, build, and distinguishing features while adapting costume and grooming to the depicted year.\n\nCHARACTER AND PERIOD — Set all concepts within 1744–1752, Vienna, Austria. Caesar is an ageless vampire throughout this period; preserve the same apparent age and identity across all four quads. Adapt ships, weapons, architecture, uniforms, clothing, furnishings, documents, and transport to the specific decade and geography of each quad. Caesar is a disciplined survivor, mariner, occult operative, and later commander: depict him actively navigating, bargaining, fighting, directing, studying, or choosing—not passively posing. Treat slavery, colonial violence, famine, war, and racial oppression with gravity rather than adventure-romance. Blood Sorcery may appear as restrained geometric skeins or seals of deep-red light. Ghosts, wraiths, and Lwa should appear as cold ash-grey translucence, shadow, breath-like mist, or possession expressed through a host’s body; do not use Chimerstry’s purple smoke language or generic fantasy beams.\n\nCAMERA AND STYLE — Keep the chronicle’s primary high-contrast black-and-white gothic-noir language with selective deep-red accents. Use mostly clear cinematic framing, with one or two purposeful low angles, high angles, restrained Dutch tilts, over-the-shoulder viewpoints, or foreground obstructions where they strengthen danger, command, confinement, or surveillance. Avoid fisheye distortion, broken anatomy, static lineups, triumphalist colonial imagery, pirate caricature, or glossy superhero posing. Keep named characters sufficiently separated for readable interaction. Reserve a calm, dark, low-detail area inside every quad for later overlays. No montage within a quad, captions, labels, readable documents, logos, extra borders, watermarks, modern anachronisms, or graphic gore.\n\nQUAD A — TOP LEFT\nCaesar enters the Prime Chantry’s monumental audience vault in salt-stained travel clothes while Jervais de la Croix presents him to severe Magisters. Stone ribs, iron doors, servants, scribes, and a geometric blood-red floor seal dwarf the mariner. Caesar remains upright and assessing, realizing that the open sea has been exchanged for a hierarchy more absolute than any captain’s.\n\nQUAD B — TOP RIGHT\nIn an alchemical chamber, Jervais de la Croix orders Caesar to complete a ritual test while apprentices and Ducheski revenants watch for failure. Caesar redirects a dangerous red skein from a boiling vessel into an iron containment circle, saving a servant the others regarded as expendable. Use a high angle through hanging instruments to emphasize surveillance and the cost of disobedient competence.\n\nQUAD C — BOTTOM LEFT\nDeep in the candlelit archives, Caesar discovers that reports of his maritime atrocities have been rewritten as useful House victories. He catches a scribe replacing one sealed page while Jervais de la Croix’s shadow falls across the stacks behind him. Caesar’s hand closes over the original record rather than a weapon, marking the moment he learns how bureaucratic obedience can imprison truth.\n\nQUAD D — BOTTOM RIGHT\nBefore departure, Jervais de la Croix gives Caesar sealed oceanic orders in a winter-dark carriage court. Caesar points to contradictory destinations on an unlabelled globe while servants load occult crates and armed guards close the gate. Jervais answers with absolute calm; Caesar accepts the mission outwardly while the low carriage-wheel viewpoint reveals his clenched free hand.",
