    splashText = "COMMON INSTRUCTIONS\nCreate one textless 1920x1080 concept-sampler image for Fomórach-22, divided into four equal 16:9 quads in a clean 2x2 grid: Quad A top-left, Quad B top-right, Quad C bottom-left, and Quad D bottom-right. Each quad must occupy exactly one quarter of the canvas and depict a different unified frozen moment suitable for later expansion into a full 1920x1080 Memoriam splash. Use a narrow neutral-black cross-shaped gutter; do not let figures, architecture, smoke, weather, or lighting cross between quads.\n\nREFERENCE INPUTS — Attach these files as actual image inputs. Attach `assets/references/style-references/general-style-reference-contact-sheet.png` only as the rendering and composition guide: cinematic gothic-noir realism, near-monochrome black, charcoal, and cold silver, hard contrast, deep negative space, wet or metallic highlights, layered atmospheric depth, and restrained blood-red accents. Do not copy its characters, Toronto locations, symbols, readable words, or exact compositions. Attach `assets/references/pc-character-references/Fomórach [Cutout].webp` as the exact identity reference for Fomórach, subject to the mortal/Embrace instruction below. Attach `assets/references/npc-character-references/Drake [Cutout].webp` as Drake’s exact appearance reference: his identity remains deliberately unknowable, with his face, head, and skin completely obscured by the same dark wraps in every depiction; never render him bare-faced or conventionally human-looking. Adapt only his outer clothing to the period while preserving the dark wrappings. Attach `assets/output/character-intro-splashes/candidates/Fomórach/fomorach-listening-wall.png`, an existing present-night title-credit asset, only as a composition reference; do not copy its exact setting, figures, or props.\n\nCHARACTER AND PERIOD — Set all concepts within 1849–1958, Toronto, Canada. Fomórach is a Nosferatu vampire throughout this period: preserve the reference identity, severe deformity, powerful frame, and apparently starved, predatory physicality across all four quads. Adapt architecture, clothing, tools, transport, weapons, and infrastructure to the exact decade and geography of each quad. Fomórach is an overlooked listener, survivor, ferocious protector, and infrastructure-minded intelligence gatherer. Show him actively eavesdropping, directing, tunneling, rescuing, hunting, confronting, or making a disastrous choice—not passively posing. Obfuscate and supernatural stealth should read through swallowed light, impossible shadow, or figures overlooking him rather than colorful invisibility effects. Hunger may appear through gaunt tension, frost-like breath, sharpened fangs, and restrained blood-red eye or mouth accents, never glamourized feeding.\n\nCAMERA AND STYLE — Keep the chronicle’s primary high-contrast black-and-white gothic-noir language with selective deep-red accents. Use mostly clear cinematic framing, with one or two purposeful low angles, high angles, restrained Dutch tilts, listening-wall viewpoints, or foreground obstructions where they strengthen secrecy, confinement, hunger, or sudden violence. Avoid fisheye distortion, broken anatomy, static lineups, monster pin-up posing, or modern horror clichés. Treat famine, colonial violence, war, homelessness, and displacement with gravity. Reserve a calm, dark, low-detail area inside every quad for later overlays. No montage within a quad, captions, labels, readable documents, logos, extra borders, watermarks, modern anachronisms, or graphic gore.\n\nQUAD A — TOP LEFT\nAfter his forced return, Fomórach kneels in an unfinished brick sewer while Drake makes him redraw a failed escape network under close scrutiny. Fomórach angrily tears up the first map, then uses the fragments to mark better routes across the wet floor. Irish migrants and fever carts pass through grates above, linking penance, the 1847 crisis, and renewed apprenticeship.\n\nQUAD B — TOP RIGHT\nDuring the Great Fire of 1904, Fomórach bursts through a service passage into a burning warehouse basement and directs trapped workers toward a sewer exit while memorizing which businesses move ledgers and valuables through the smoke. Drake appears at another tunnel mouth with orders to protect Camarilla assets. Fire-red accents, collapsing brick, water, and steam make rescue and intelligence morally inseparable.\n\nQUAD C — BOTTOM LEFT\nIn a Prohibition-era utility chamber, Fomórach catches bootleggers using his tunnels while police raid the street above. He pins one smuggler against a telephone relay rack, redirects contraband toward a hidden clan cache, and listens through a pipe to investigators overhead. Brick sewers, streetcar cables, electrical machinery, and red indicator lights show the undercity becoming an information machine.\n\nQUAD D — BOTTOM RIGHT\nIn the Cold War 1950s, Fomórach and Drake stand over a layered command table inside a postwar service corridor as transit maps, telephone lines, immigration records, and Sabbat incidents converge. Drake finally hands him authority over a section of the network; Fomórach points toward the Mojave lead that will soon call him away. Keep the room physical and analog rather than modern digital.",
    npcs = {
